Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,
I calculated LOD scores using scanone, with addcovar and intcovar parameters. If i understand correctly, this gives me a strictly genetic effect, clean of the effect of the environment (both additively and interactively). I would like to know the size of only the additive effect and only the interactive GxE effect, to be able to get a clearer picture of what i am looking at.

So my question is, what is the most reliable way to calculate (or extract from existing data) these effects, that  is clear and separate from the genetic effect? (i am assuming this is either in format of LOD scores or percent variance explained..?)

Many thanks in advance

From u09858b6ac9e567bc at groups.google.com  Wed Jul 15 10:54:19 2020
From: u09858b6ac9e567bc at groups.google.com (Noa BL)
Date: Wed, 15 Jul 2020 10:54:19 +0000
Subject: Suspiciously high LOD score
Message-ID: <9rI3SrDSOB4.D8yh2b7eBQAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/9rI3SrDSOB4/m/D8yh2b7eBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,
For some of my potential QTLs i am receiving extremely high LOD scores (>200). This calculates into very high percentage of variance explained and that is also very suspicious considering my known-as-noisy experimental setting. For these reasons i suspect it is an artefact but am not sure what causes it.
I use scanone with method=ehk.
What are some factors that may cause this that I should look into?

Many thanks in advance

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Wed Jul 15 11:35:16 2020
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
